<p id="p29">Moving on to other performance-related changes, a redesigned vapor chamber with three times the surface area helps deliver up to 40% better sustained performance than iPhone 17 Pro. Given Apple’s focus on gaming and AI for these phones, I look forward to comparing heat dissipation between my 17 Pro and 18 Pro.</p>
<p id="p30">As for battery life, Apple quotes up to 36 hours of video playback for the Pro and a whopping <em>45 hours</em> for the Pro Max – the longest battery life ever in an iPhone. (There’s a caveat: those figures apply to eSIM-only models; versions with a physical SIM get two hours less.) Also new this year: both phones can charge to 50% in around 15 minutes with a compatible 60W-capable USB-C adapter that supports Adjustable Voltage Supply, whereas MagSafe and Qi2 still top out at 25W.</p>
<h4 id="pricing-and-availability">Pricing and Availability</h4>
<p id="p31">The iPhone 18 Pro starts at $1,199 in the U.S., while the Pro Max starts at $1,299 (a $100 price hike compared to last year for both versions).</p>
<p id="p32">Both models come in black, silver, glacier, and the new burgundy (which I think looks great), with storage ranging from 256GB to 2TB. Preorders open September 12 at 5 a.m. Pacific, with availability on September 18.</p>
<h3 id="iphone-duo">iPhone Duo</h3>
<p id="p33">Alright, let’s now talk about the iPhone I’ve been dreaming of for the past several years.</p>
<p id="p34">The basic idea is easy enough to understand: the iPhone Duo is a foldable device with a passport-style design that has a smaller screen on the outside, and can be opened to reveal a larger one. You’ve seen foldables before. This one goes from a 5.4-inch display that opens up to reveal a 7.6-inch one inside – effectively <em>almost</em> reaching the dimensions of an iPad mini. When taking a look at pictures and videos of the iPhone Duo in portrait and landscape, it’s hard not to think of Apple’s diminutive tablet.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0113562x-1788995664288-4318e68404.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p36">What’s more interesting, however, is what Apple has done with the iPhone Duo’s software to take advantage of that extra space. First off, it’s important to clarify that **the iPhone Duo does not run iPadOS **and it is not an iPad when you open it. The iPhone Duo runs a special version of iOS that was “inspired” by certain elements of the iPad – such as Split View multitasking, or inter-app drag and drop – but which also introduces new ideas never seen before in either iPadOS or iOS, such as tabletop layouts, Liquid Glass controls on the side of the screen, and more.</p>
<h4 id="design-displays-andapple-pencil">Design, Displays, and…Apple Pencil?</h4>
<p id="p37">First, the hardware. The Duo is made of mirror-polished titanium and weighs 254 grams. Closed, it measures 117.8 × 84.1 × 11.3 mm; open, it’s 117.8 × 164.6 × 5.2 mm. Both OLED displays support ProMotion up to 120Hz, Always-On, and 3,000 nits of peak outdoor brightness. According to Apple, the inner screen gives you 50% more display area than an iPhone 18 Pro Max, and it supports any orientation.</p>
<p id="p38">The inner display, just like high-end iPad Pro models, has a nano-texture finish, which has been designed to reduce glare and make the crease less visible. Note the distinction here: Apple isn’t saying there’s <em>no</em> crease; they’re saying the material “minimizes crease visibility”. Judging from early pictures and hands-on videos posted online, it appears that the crease will be slightly visible when the inner display is viewed from certain angles.</p>
<p id="p39">During the event’s video, Apple was also very proud to show off the hinge mechanism they designed for the iPhone Duo, which is comprised of over 100 components that support the center of the display:</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0006252x-1788991609946-111e32c4fb.png" alt="Johny Srouji’s salary, visualized."><p class="image-caption">Johny Srouji’s salary, visualized.</p></div>
<p id="p41">Ceramic Shield protects the back of the device, with Ceramic Shield 2 on the outer display. The Duo also carries an IP68 rating for dust and water resistance. These are reassuring details in theory, but, as with any foldable, durability will have to be tested over time.</p>
<p id="p42">Now, what is arguably a pain point of this first-generation iPhone Duo is the lack of Face ID. Instead, Apple has put Touch ID in the side button, similar to the iPad mini, so it works regardless of whether the phone is open or closed. After nine years of Face ID on the iPhone, it’s going to be tough to get reacquainted with Touch ID; I hope that Apple’s placement of the button and support for multiple fingerprints will alleviate those concerns. We’ll see.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0017102x-1788992250235-2165dac679.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p44">You can also unlock the Duo with an Apple Watch, which I assume is going to be similar to how this feature works with a nearby paired Mac. If that method works well, there’s a chance we won’t miss Face ID’s simplicity much, after all.</p>
<p id="p45">Lastly, in a first for an iPhone, both screens will support the Apple Pencil (USB-C model). This is what it looks like:</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0023292x-1788992631182-471ec895e4.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p47">Sorry, wrong file. This is what it looks like:</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0025442x-1788992777945-85598f04f0.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p49">In the announcement video, Apple said Pencil integration will be available “later this year”.</p>
<h4 id="cameras-2">Cameras</h4>
<p id="p50">The camera system is another area where the Duo differs from the Pro models. In the Duo, you get a 48MP Fusion Main camera with a fixed ƒ/1.6 aperture, plus a 48MP Fusion Ultra Wide with an ƒ/2.2 aperture and macro support. There’s no dedicated Telephoto lens, although the Main camera offers an optical-quality 2x option. The outer display has a 12MP Center Stage camera, while a separate FaceTime camera sits underneath the inner display and records video at up to 1080p and 60 fps. From Apple’s demo videos, it appears that the area surrounding the under-display camera will look slightly dimmer; I’d like to understand if this is something that will be visible at all times, or if it’s a UI decision to indicate where the camera actually is.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0035082x-1788993332155-6c44e9406b.png" alt="The under-display camera “lights up” in software during a FaceTime call."><p class="image-caption">The under-display camera “lights up” in software during a FaceTime call.</p></div>
<p id="p52">What the Duo offers is also a series of features designed around the idea of having two screens. With <strong>Duo Preview</strong>, the person you’re photographing can see the live camera view on the outer display and adjust their pose or framing if they want to. You can use that screen to take selfies with the rear cameras, too. <strong>Kid Cue</strong> takes a more playful approach, putting Peanuts animations (and ideally something else, too?) on the outer display to get children to look at the camera while you’re framing a shot.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0037522x-1788993513675-8dec603428.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p54">With the Duo propped up thanks to one of its “halves”, the new** Smart Take** feature uses on-device AI to recognize when people are posing and automatically take their photo. And <strong>Duo FaceTime</strong> lets someone sitting with you join a call using the outer screen and camera, rather than having everyone squeeze into the same shot.</p>
<p id="p55">We’ve seen some of these features on other foldables before, but I think Apple is offering a more complete, consistent package with these different “postures” for the iPhone Duo and the Camera app from the get-go.</p>
<h4 id="performance-and-battery-2">Performance and Battery</h4>
<p id="p56">The Duo runs the same A20 Pro as the Pro models, paired with its own vapor chamber.</p>
<p id="p57">Battery life is a little more complicated to describe when a phone has two displays. Apple quotes up to 31 hours of video playback on the inner screen or 44 hours on the outer one. The company also gives a separate estimate of up to 24 hours of “typical use”, based on using the two screens equally. Obviously, how you divide your time between the displays will make a difference here.</p>
<p id="p58">For charging, you’re looking at up to 50% in around 20 minutes with a 60W-capable wired adapter, or around 30 minutes with a compatible MagSafe charger and adapter. MagSafe and Qi2 both support up to 25W.</p>
<h4 id="ios-and-multitasking">iOS and Multitasking</h4>
<p id="p59">As a longtime iPad user, this is the part of the Duo’s story that interests me most. And I feel that, as someone who’s spent over a decade witnessing the iPad’s multitasking evolution, I’ve been waiting for this kind of iPhone model to arrive, and I can’t wait to get my hands on it.</p>
<p id="p60">Apple will ship the iPhone Duo with iOS 27.1, and the iOS interface has been adapted to function as a hybrid of a miniaturized iPadOS and an enlarged iOS that moves several controls off to the side of the screen.</p>
<p id="p61">For starters, Lock Screen controls, the Home Screen dock, and app navigation are moving to the side to make more vertical room for content. This makes sense given the device’s atypically short aspect ratio; as you can see from one of Apple’s shots, it seems like bottom tab bars will automatically be anchored on the right side of the screen and displayed vertically.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0049132x-1788994450193-dfaf0607b4.png" alt="The Music tab bar on iPhone Duo."><p class="image-caption">The Music tab bar on iPhone Duo.</p></div>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0049362x-1788994453106-b80a284beb.png" alt="More toolbars on the Duo."><p class="image-caption">More toolbars on the Duo.</p></div>
<p id="p64">When you open the Duo, the Home Screen displays two pages at the same time, which you can customize with widgets. It’s unclear, at the moment, whether the dock will support adding more than four app icons to it (my money is, sadly, on “no”).</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0049542x-1788994457640-f825b48f24.png" alt="The Home Screen."><p class="image-caption">The Home Screen.</p></div>
<p id="p66">Even the Dynamic Island has been rethought as a vertical element along the side of both displays.</p>
<p id="p67">Thus, the idea here is that iOS should follow along as you fold, rotate, or flip the Duo. This kind of layout flexibility is unprecedented for iOS, and when you factor in the fact that iOS 27 will be able to measure the angle to which the Duo is open in real time, it opens the door to some fascinating software design decisions. For example, iOS can dynamically adapt to a not-fully-open Duo and change the keyboard layout to move the keys away from the folding area:</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0053022x-1788994497075-b81c054bb7.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p69">Or, it can put the keyboard on the part of the screen that’s sitting flat on a surface:</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0050282x-1788994466984-2668fc8b43.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p71">Or, you can turn the device to portrait mode, and type on it as if it were a smaller iPad mini:</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0052292x-1788994488222-411e148358.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p73">Then there’s Split View, which brings <strong>two apps side by side to the iPhone for the first time</strong>. You can also open two windows of the same app (like on an iPad) and save pairs of apps to return to later (like on Android). At the moment, it seems like the only way to add apps to Split View is from the Home Screen on one side of the screen; I’d love to know if dragging apps from Spotlight will also be supported.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0050132x-1788994463858-d8d24cb1c4.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0051462x-1788994478585-6252c5ee53.png" alt="Starting Split View from the Home Screen."><p class="image-caption">Starting Split View from the Home Screen.</p></div>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0052172x-1788994482972-fe30dfb1c9.png" alt="Creating app pairs to come back to later."><p class="image-caption">Creating app pairs to come back to later.</p></div>
<p id="p77">Another nice touch: <strong>StandBy no longer requires a charger</strong> on the Duo.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0051192x-1788994473736-18a86ad2c5.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-0053182x-1788994492592-4bb5690fdc.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p80">Prop the phone up, and you can use either display for widgets, photos, or a clock. There are new Calendar and Weather faces, Shared Albums in the Photos face, and more customization options, too.</p>
<h4 id="pricing-and-availability-2">Pricing and Availability</h4>
<p id="p81">The iPhone Duo starts at $1,999 in the U.S. and comes in night sky and star white, with 256GB, 512GB, 1TB, or 2TB of storage. Preorders open October 16 at 5 a.m. Pacific, with availability on October 23 and a second regional rollout on October 30.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/apple-iphone-duo-folio-taupe-260909-bigjpglarge-2x-1788994658606-07ca2a9f51.jpg" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/09/apple-iphone-duo-case-sand-260909-bigjpglarge-2x-1788994660262-a078730384.jpg" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p84">Apple is also selling a $79 Duo Case in navy blue and sand, plus a $129 Duo Folio with Kickstand in navy blue and taupe. The latter adds an adjustable stand for reading, watching videos, or FaceTime; both support MagSafe and can be ordered starting October 16. The full hardware specifications are available on <a href="https://www.apple.com/iphone-duo/specs/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apple’s website</a>.</p>
<hr id="hr85"><p id="p86">As you can probably tell from this story, I’m going to get an iPhone Duo as soon as possible, and it’s going to be my primary device for the foreseeable future. I’ve been waiting for Apple to make an iPhone inspired by the iPad for a long time.</p>
<p id="p87">From what I’ve seen today, I think Apple is right: the iPhone Duo truly feels like the most transformational iPhone since the original.</p>
<p id="p88">October 23 can’t come soon enough.</p>
